+- **The token**: 32 bytes from the OS random source, base64url, 43 characters. It does not expire; the owner revokes. A native client sends it only as a bearer header over the pinned connection, never in a URL. A browser never holds it in script: its `/pair` asks for `"transport": "cookie"`, and the reply leaves `token` out of the body and sets `__Host-sb-<server_id>=<token>; Max-Age=34560000; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict`. The name carries the server id because cookies ignore ports: two instances on one Mac share a browser's jar for the address, and each reads only its own name (every other cookie is ignored); the id survives a key rotation. A request's credential is the bearer when there is one, else that cookie. A request authenticated by the cookie alone that is not a `GET`, or is an upgrade, must carry `Origin: https://<its Host header>` or is refused 403 `origin`; `SameSite=Strict` already keeps the cookie off cross-site requests, and this closes a page on another port of the same address. A cookie is not scoped by port, so another HTTPS service on the same address receives it.
